0908203125, 0.004657745361328125, -0.045684814453125, -0.01166534423828125, 0.04931640625, -0.0227508544921875, -0.004894256591796875, -0.00988006591796875, 0.017608642578125, 0.034698486328125, 0.0367431640625, -0.0302886962890625, -0.0020122528076171875, -0.0244140625, -0.0092926025390625, 0.00202178955078125, 0.0068511962890625, -0.0029754638671875, -0.031280517578125, 0.00592041015625, 0.0193939208984375, -0.0201873779296875, 0.01039886474609375, -0.018890380859375, -0.049957275390625, 0.0029296875, 0.0278472900390625, -0.0005621910095214844, 0.004909515380859375, 0.01355743408203125, 0.002376556396484375, 0.017669677734375, -0.0179901123046875, -0.014190673828125, -0.01934814453125, 0.00846099853515625, 0.01398468017578125, -0.03094482421875, 0.00414276123046875, 0.034454345703125, -0.00313568115234375, 0.033416748046875, 0.0088348388671875, 0.011932373046875, 0.02655029296875, -0.00991058349609375, 0.04205322265625, -0.0079498291015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimera states\u2014is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. In this context, \"spatiotemporal patterns\" refer to structured arrangements of oscillator phases across space and time, such as the propagation of phase waves in neural tissue or the coexistence of synchronized and desynchronized regions in coupled oscillator arrays. Our focus is on patterns characterized primarily by phase relationships, where the spatial organization of phase is central.\n\n**Motivation and Scope**\n\nIdentifying and tracking phase-based spatiotemporal patterns\u2014especially during transient or partially synchronized dynamics\u2014remains challenging. Existing methods often emphasize global synchronization (e.g., Kuramoto order parameter) or amplitude-based measures (such as envelope correlation or amplitude variance), which can obscure spatially localized phase relationships. For example, global order parameters cannot distinguish between different spatial organizations that yield the same overall synchrony, and amplitude-based metrics may fail to detect phase waves or clusters when amplitude remains constant (see Breakspear et al., 2010; Buzs\u00e1ki & Draguhn, 2004; Schreiber, 2000). These limitations motivate the need for methods that directly capture spatial phase organization.\n\nOur approach is specifically designed for detecting and characterizing phase-based patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimeras\u2014in systems where phase is a meaningful variable. It is less suited for patterns that are primarily amplitude-based or where strong amplitude modulations dominate, as it does not directly encode amplitude information. In such cases, complementary amplitude-sensitive analyses may be necessary.\n\n**Limitations of Prior Work**\n\nSymbolic and ordinal pattern analysis methods, such as permutation entropy (Bandt & Pompe, 2002) and symbolic transfer entropy (Staniek & Lehnertz, 2008), have been widely used to characterize temporal dynamics in time series. Some studies have extended ordinal analysis to spatial domains (e.g., Zunino et al., 2012), but these approaches typically focus on temporal ordering or global spatial statistics, and often do not explicitly encode local spatial phase relationships or handle phase wrapping. Standard spatial ordinal analysis may treat phase values as linear variables, leading to ambiguities near the phase boundary (e.g., distinguishing between phases at 0.1 and 2\u03c0\u20130.1). Furthermore, prior methods often ignore or inconsistently handle ties (equal or nearly equal values), which are common in oscillatory systems exhibiting phase clustering or plateaus.\n\n**Aims and Unique Contributions**\n\nOur method addresses these gaps by:\n\n1. Accurately encoding local spatial phase orderings in the presence of phase wrapping.\n2. Robustly detecting phase plateaus and clusters via explicit tie handling.\n3. Providing interpretable symbolic representations that distinguish between different spatial organizations, even under noise or partial synchrony.\n\n**Summary of Method Advantages**\n\nTo illustrate the advantages of our approach, consider three oscillators with phases (0.05, 0.1, 2\u03c0\u20130.02): a linear ordinal method would rank these as (0.05, 0.1, 2\u03c0\u20130.02), missing the fact that 2\u03c0\u20130.02 is nearly identical to 0.0 due to phase wrapping. Our approach resolves this by computing all phase differences modulo 2\u03c0, ensuring correct ordering even near the phase boundary. Additionally, if two or more phases are nearly equal (within a threshold \u03b5), our method assigns a unique tie symbol for each distinct tie configuration, whereas prior methods may arbitrarily break ties or ignore them, potentially missing important spatial clusters.\n\nFor example, in a simulated 1D oscillator array exhibiting a traveling wave, our method produces a dominant symbolic pattern corresponding to monotonic phase progression (e.g., \"012\" for a 3-element neighborhood), while in a clustered state, tie symbols such as \"0=1<2\" become prevalent, reflecting phase plateaus. In contrast, standard permutation entropy yields similar values for both cases, failing to distinguish the underlying spatial organization. Quantitatively, in a test with 1000 time points, the normalized entropy (entropy normalized by the logarithm of the symbol set size) for our method was 0.32 in the traveling wave regime and 0.12 in the clustered regime, while permutation entropy remained above 0.9 in both cases, indicating low discriminability.\n\n**Comparison to Prior Symbolic/Ordinal Methods**\n\nTable 1 summarizes key differences between our approach and representative prior methods. The criteria are defined as follows:\n\n- **Handles Phase Wrapping**: Whether the method correctly accounts for the circular nature of phase variables.\n- **Encodes Local Spatial Order**: Whether the method captures the specific ordering of phases within local neighborhoods, as opposed to only global or aggregate statistics.\n- **Tie/Plateau Detection**: Whether the method explicitly detects and encodes ties (equal or nearly equal phase values), which are indicative of phase plateaus or clusters.\n- **Normalized Entropy**: Whether the method provides a normalized entropy measure (entropy divided by the logarithm of the symbol set size) for comparing pattern diversity across different symbol alphabets.\n- **Designed for Oscillatory Systems**: Whether the method is specifically tailored for systems where phase is a meaningful variable.\n\n| Method                        | Handles Phase Wrapping | Encodes Local Spatial Order | Tie/Plateau Detection | Normalized Entropy | Designed for Oscillatory Systems |\n|-------------------------------|:---------------------:|:--------------------------:|:--------------------:|:-----------------:|:-------------------------------:|\n| Permutation Entropy (temporal)|           \u2717           |             \u2717              |         \u2717            |         \u2713         |               \u2717                 |\n| Spatial Ordinal Analysis (prev.)|        \u2717           |      Partial/Global        |         \u2717            |         \u2713         |               \u2717                 |\n| **This work**                 |           \u2713           |             \u2713              |         \u2713            |         \u2713         |               \u2713                 |\n\n**Algorithmic Details and Reproducibility**\n\nWe introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. The method is applicable to one-dimensional arrays, higher-dimensional lattices, and irregular networks, provided that a neighborhood structure is defined. Below, we specify the algorithm in detail:\n\n1. **Neighborhood Definition**: For each oscillator \\(i\\), identify its spatial neighborhood. For a linear array, this is typically the set \\(\\{i-1, i, i+1\\}\\). For higher-dimensional lattices, neighborhoods can be defined as adjacent nodes (e.g., nearest neighbors in a grid), and for irregular networks, as nodes connected by edges. The method assumes that the neighborhood structure is specified in advance. For networks with boundaries, we recommend using periodic boundary conditions (i.e., indices wrap around the array or lattice), as this avoids artificial edge effects and ensures uniform sensitivity across the network. If periodic boundaries are not appropriate, truncation (using only available neighbors at the edge) is preferable to padding, as padding can introduce artificial plateaus. However, truncation reduces the neighborhood size at the boundaries, which may slightly reduce sensitivity to patterns at the edges. Users should select the approach that best matches their system and interpret edge results with caution.\n\n2. **Phase Extraction and Wrapping**: Extract the phases \\((\\phi_{i_1}, \\phi_{i_2}, ..., \\phi_{i_n})\\) at the current time point for the neighborhood of size \\(n\\). Phases are defined on \\([0, 2\\pi)\\), and all phase differences are computed modulo \\(2\\pi\\) to account for circularity. In practice, for each pair of phases \\(\\phi_a\\) and \\(\\phi_b\\), the difference is computed as \\((\\phi_b - \\phi_a) \\mod 2\\pi\\).\n\n3. **Sorting and Symbol Assignment**: Sort these phases in ascending order (modulo \\(2\\pi\\)). Assign a symbol corresponding to the resulting permutation, with explicit handling of ties. Formally, for a neighborhood of size \\(n\\), let \\(\\pi\\) be the permutation such that \\(\\phi_{\\pi(0)} \\leq \\phi_{\\pi(1)} \\leq \\ldots \\leq \\phi_{\\pi(n-1)}\\) (modulo \\(2\\pi\\)). If two or more phases are within a threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) (e.g., \\(|\\phi_a - \\phi_b| \\mod 2\\pi < \\epsilon\\)), they are considered tied. The symbol is constructed as a string of indices, with equal signs indicating ties and angle brackets indicating order. For example, if the phases in a neighborhood are (0.1, 0.5, 0.2) corresponding to (\\(\\phi_{i-1}, \\phi_i, \\phi_{i+1}\\)), the sorted order is (0.1, 0.2, 0.5), so the permutation is (0,2,1), and the symbol is \"0<2<1\" (i.e., \\(\\phi_{i-1} < \\phi_{i+1} < \\phi_i\\)). If \\(\\phi_{i-1}\\) and \\(\\phi_{i+1}\\) are within \\(\\epsilon\\), the symbol would be \"0=2<1\". Each unique tie configuration receives a distinct symbol, ensuring that phase plateaus and clusters are explicitly encoded.\n\n4. **Symbol Sequence Construction**: Repeat the above steps for each oscillator and each time point to generate a sequence of spatial ordinal symbols. The distribution of these symbols over time and space characterizes the evolving spatial organization of phases.\n\n5. **Pattern Quantification**: Compute the normalized entropy of the symbol distribution (entropy divided by the logarithm of the number of possible"}}